openCFW licensing boundary
==========================

The root MIT License applies to openCFW-authored software and documentation
that do not declare a different license.

Additional MIT license grant
----------------------------

To the extent that copyright in a file is held by the openCFW contributors,
the contributors offer that original contribution under the root MIT License.
Files that declare `MIT OR GPL` also continue to permit MIT use; their GPL
option is not removed by this grant.

This additional grant does not apply to material derived from another GPL
work, including the upstream QP/C sources (submodule
`third-party/upstream/qpc`) and the g2flash sources (submodule
`third-party/reference/g2flash`). Earlier g2flash-derived overlay files were
removed on 2026-09-29; they remain under GPL in the Git history.

Those files remain under their stated upstream GPL terms. A combined firmware
image containing GPL-covered code must satisfy the GPL terms applicable to the
combined work; the additional MIT grant applies to the separable openCFW-owned
contributions and does not weaken those obligations.

It does not replace or override a license, copyright notice, provenance
record, or redistribution restriction attached to an individual file,
component, dependency, evidence artifact, or firmware payload. In particular:

- Code in submodules under third-party/ and in fetched vendor archives remains
  under the upstream license shipped with that dependency.
- Files with an SPDX-License-Identifier or an adjacent component license remain
  under that stated license, including Apache-2.0, BSD, GPL, MIT, and Zlib
  material, subject to the additional grant above for original openCFW-owned
  contributions.
- Official Even Realities firmware payloads, retained compatibility bytes,
  extracted binary data, and other vendor-proprietary inputs are not licensed
  under the root MIT License and are not tracked. The existing development
  history contains tracked donor/build artifacts and is not a public
  distribution boundary.
- Reverse-engineering evidence and captured vendor artifacts do not become
  MIT-licensed merely because they are stored beside project-authored tooling.

Component-level NOTICE, LICENSE and PROVENANCE files, and each
submodule's own licence, identify the applicable upstream terms and source boundary.
When a component-level statement conflicts with the repository default, the
more specific component or file statement controls.

The openCFW name does not grant rights to third-party trademarks, product
names, firmware signing keys, or proprietary services.

R1 attributable memory/string provider candidate
------------------------------------------------
The unchanged musl v1.2.5 memory/string subset under
third-party/upstream/musl-memory-1.2.5 retains musl's MIT license and attribution
in COPYRIGHT. Its provenance.json records the upstream pin and file hashes.
This candidate is not represented as the original firmware's Arm runtime.

R1 attributable integer runtime provider candidate
-------------------------------------------------
The unchanged LLVM compiler-rt 20.1.8 unsigned division source is reused from
third-party/upstream/llvm-compiler-rt-format-20.1.8. The applicable compiler-rt
license (Apache 2.0 with LLVM exceptions and retained legacy notices) is in
r1/source-config/runtime/LLVM-COMPILER-RT-LICENSE.TXT; the immutable upstream
commit and authenticated file hashes are in llvm_integer_provenance.json in
that directory. R1-specific EABI glue and stock zero-divisor/shift behavior
are separate project source. This is not original Arm runtime source identity.

R1 BAE8 service-send adaptation
------------------------------
r1/components/board/native_bae8_send.c retains Nordic's SDK NUS copyright and
license notice. It adapts the NUS-shaped path to authenticated R1 dual
notification, logging and return-policy contracts. SDK HVX structures and SVC
identifiers remain attributable to the pinned nRF5 SDK 17.1.0. The build report
pins its SDK headers and NUS reference source against the SDK archive. Neither
the full proprietary service object nor SoftDevice source is supplied by this
adaptation; those remain separate reconstruction gates.
